WEBVTT

1
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.820
Tim Nunamaker: Macie… Hi, Lisa Can you say?

2
00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.030
Maciej: I don't think so.

3
00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.829
Tim Nunamaker: Maybe we can just wait another minute and get started.

4
00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:20.300
Tim Nunamaker: Alright, I'll just jump in. We'll have some time, maybe after I go through these slides, where we can chat a little bit about the material, or whatever else comes to mind.

5
00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:23.149
Tim Nunamaker: Let me just throw my screen up here.

6
00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:44.139
Tim Nunamaker: You guys see that okay?

7
00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:47.320
Tim Nunamaker: Awesome.

8
00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:54.150
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so this is our third session going over PDPP and kind of introducing

9
00:05:54.350 --> 00:06:02.280
Tim Nunamaker: the initial version. This is in advance of GDC, where we'll announce, V0.1.

10
00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:06.370
Tim Nunamaker: And we're happy to have you guys here, and

11
00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:09.460
Tim Nunamaker: Share some notes and get some feedback.

12
00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:16.879
Tim Nunamaker: Today, we're talking about… Grants. Basically, how is consent to data?

13
00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:20.859
Tim Nunamaker: Stored and used. And then how is the data served?

14
00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:26.519
Tim Nunamaker: In the first session, we went over the architecture and overall introduction of PDPP.

15
00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.420
Tim Nunamaker: And last week, we talked about

16
00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:33.989
Tim Nunamaker: How does a client request access to data, and how is data modeled?

17
00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:40.560
Tim Nunamaker: And those sessions are recorded and documented in the PDPP GitHub repo.

18
00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:42.270
Tim Nunamaker: If you want to find that.

19
00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:45.490
Tim Nunamaker: Next week, we will talk about

20
00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:50.259
Tim Nunamaker: governance, and I think Art will be leading that session.

21
00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:54.949
Tim Nunamaker: from the Bonnet Foundation, and we'll talk about some of the key open questions.

22
00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.810
Tim Nunamaker: for the current state of PDPP.

23
00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:06.960
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so last week, basically the gist of the material was A client

24
00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:15.899
Tim Nunamaker: would request access to data with a selection request. That selection request basically would be interpreted by

25
00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:20.260
Tim Nunamaker: The server containing the user's data and authorizing it.

26
00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:24.330
Tim Nunamaker: in the render a consent screen. So think of, like, OAuth.

27
00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:27.420
Tim Nunamaker: Such and such application wants to access

28
00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:34.859
Tim Nunamaker: This application… your account with this application, but there would be additional details about accessing the user's data.

29
00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:40.110
Tim Nunamaker: And some of the key points there were that

30
00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:43.999
Tim Nunamaker: Data could either be modeled as being mutable or immutable.

31
00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:49.330
Tim Nunamaker: Emails in your inbox that are really old, they're probably not changing over time.

32
00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:53.000
Tim Nunamaker: And so we can buy some optimizations that way.

33
00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:57.519
Tim Nunamaker: We don't have any canonical data models, so…

34
00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:04.780
Tim Nunamaker: Unlike some other standards, we basically try to meet the data where it is in the form that it exists, which

35
00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:18.310
Tim Nunamaker: Comes with a benefit of being able to express the data in sort of its full form, and some trade-offs around, aggregating data across different data sources may require a little bit of additional effort to make them uniform.

36
00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:24.019
Tim Nunamaker: And… We also took a pretty opinionated stance that

37
00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:32.510
Tim Nunamaker: we want the UIs that are showing consent to have semantics for not just what the protocol can guarantee, but

38
00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:41.590
Tim Nunamaker: Any sort of terms of service that the user wants to provide, to clients accessing their data, so that those commitments can be attributed.

39
00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:52.779
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so… Today, we're talking about, really, grants as, like, the key artifact of the entire PDPP standard.

40
00:08:52.950 --> 00:09:07.490
Tim Nunamaker: A grant is fairly simple, conceptually. It's just a record of, at this time, this subject, a user, gave permission for a client to access this particular

41
00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:08.980
Tim Nunamaker: Piece of their data.

42
00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:14.120
Tim Nunamaker: And specifically, grants are scoped to specific data source.

43
00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.500
Tim Nunamaker: So, if you want to…

44
00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:21.870
Tim Nunamaker: Serve both email data and music listening data, you would need, you know, two different grants.

45
00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:31.809
Tim Nunamaker: But that can be handled by the server. So the grant is meant to be fairly narrow, and it records why was access granted.

46
00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:41.309
Tim Nunamaker: The terms of access. Is it one-time access? Is it continuous access? Is there a time frame for the access? And then…

47
00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:52.100
Tim Nunamaker: There's detail about specifically what data can be accessed, and that's based on the data model streams and the fields within those streams.

48
00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:59.230
Tim Nunamaker: And… Within streams, we can also apply time constraints, so it could be something like

49
00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:05.550
Tim Nunamaker: This is a grant for access to bank statements over the year 2025.

50
00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:14.549
Tim Nunamaker: And this grant is versioned so that we can always refer back to

51
00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:18.910
Tim Nunamaker: Like, which version of the protocol, and which version of…

52
00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:23.460
Tim Nunamaker: The data source declaration the grant applies to.

53
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:34.720
Tim Nunamaker: So, over time, maybe the client changes its brand, and the logo changes, that's metadata that's stored in the grant.

54
00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:41.050
Tim Nunamaker: The grant is meant to be immutable, it's a historical record, and…

55
00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:45.099
Tim Nunamaker: So maybe there's some drift with how things change in the future, but…

56
00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:49.070
Tim Nunamaker: We really want the grant to be immutable and also capture

57
00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:55.620
Tim Nunamaker: Anything that's… anything that's dynamic, for example, a view that says.

58
00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:59.689
Tim Nunamaker: I want to access the user's basic profile information.

59
00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:05.470
Tim Nunamaker: If that dynamic Basic profile definition changes over time.

60
00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:15.009
Tim Nunamaker: Actually, when the grant is created, it needs to be resolved into specific fields. And so, if the definition of basic profile changes in a future version, the grant

61
00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:18.849
Tim Nunamaker: Still states exactly which fields it applied to.

62
00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:22.579
Tim Nunamaker: The grant can actually change

63
00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:26.179
Tim Nunamaker: In the sense that the user can narrow permissions.

64
00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:32.129
Tim Nunamaker: So, we could say we're going to stop granting access to

65
00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:43.360
Tim Nunamaker: specific fields in the grant can still be used to access the other fields without having to reissue a new grant, but it can't go the other direction. We can't say.

66
00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:53.360
Tim Nunamaker: This data source has some new features with new data, and we're just going to assume that access should be granted to those new fields.

67
00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.929
Tim Nunamaker: Maybe, maybe some new profile fields were added.

68
00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:58.919
Tim Nunamaker: That would require a new grant.

69
00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:07.169
Tim Nunamaker: When grants are revoked, so a user can revoke a grant.

70
00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:14.029
Tim Nunamaker: Obviously, we can't go out into the clients and say, give us the data back, or prove that you've deleted the data.

71
00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:19.169
Tim Nunamaker: So revoking, access really just cuts off future access.

72
00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:26.160
Tim Nunamaker: However, grants do contain Some policies, like, what is the data retention policy?

73
00:12:26.330 --> 00:12:33.720
Tim Nunamaker: That the client is committing to. And this is similar to how OAuth token revocation works, and…

74
00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:44.550
Tim Nunamaker: I think the goal here is basically to just provide the scaffolding with which, sort of lawful enforcement can be applied if needed later on.

75
00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:52.890
Tim Nunamaker: This is maybe just a little bit of a lesson in

76
00:12:53.120 --> 00:13:00.890
Tim Nunamaker: OAuth, and a specific choice that we're making in relation to revoking access to data.

77
00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:09.060
Tim Nunamaker: But I think it's worth mentioning that instead of using JWTs, where…

78
00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:19.700
Tim Nunamaker: a token is signed and can be inspected, and the server could verify, like, okay, yes, I granted,

79
00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:24.900
Tim Nunamaker: like, I signed this in the past, and it proves that access has been granted.

80
00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:34.549
Tim Nunamaker: Therefore, I don't need to go look at anything in my database, I don't need to make another round trip, I'm just going to continue giving access. We chose not to do that because

81
00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:39.539
Tim Nunamaker: Then the user can't revoke access and have that revocation

82
00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:52.899
Tim Nunamaker: go into force immediately. So, introspection is required when a client requests access to data with their token, with their access token. That access token gets checked to see if it's still valid.

83
00:13:53.240 --> 00:14:05.530
Tim Nunamaker: We allow a 60-second cache, but no more than that. So, if you revoke access, you have a guarantee that within 60 seconds, clients will not be permitted to fetch new data.

84
00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.490
Tim Nunamaker: So now we can talk a little bit about…

85
00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:19.510
Tim Nunamaker: The query language, like, how does a client actually ask for data that it's been granted access to?

86
00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:26.749
Tim Nunamaker: And… The full detail of all this is in the stand… is in the spec, but it's basically…

87
00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:33.420
Tim Nunamaker: Listing what streams the user has that you've been granted access to.

88
00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:35.969
Tim Nunamaker: Getting metadata about those streams.

89
00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:38.729
Tim Nunamaker: Listing records within a stream.

90
00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:42.379
Tim Nunamaker: Or grabbing an individual record.

91
00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:46.340
Tim Nunamaker: Or if a record has some kind of blob,

92
00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:58.330
Tim Nunamaker: defined, like, an image or a video at some other URL, a way to go grab that as well. So that can be, like, an attachment from an email posted on another server.

93
00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:00.159
Tim Nunamaker: Or on the same server.

94
00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:03.109
Tim Nunamaker: And it's important to note that

95
00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:16.829
Tim Nunamaker: Everything that the client sees when they query for things like, tell me what streams are available for the user. Those are always filtered down through what the grant allows.

96
00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:18.300
Tim Nunamaker: And…

97
00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:24.579
Tim Nunamaker: We actually don't have a way at this time for clients to just go look up what is all the data.

98
00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:33.600
Tim Nunamaker: That a user has that they could potentially grant me access to. We may add some features in the future where users can sort of permit

99
00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:39.270
Tim Nunamaker: additional metadata queries, But at the moment.

100
00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:51.539
Tim Nunamaker: Basically, the only thing a client can do is ask the user, please give me access to your data, and the user will indicate which access should be granted. And at that point, the client can learn what data is available.

101
00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:57.399
Tim Nunamaker: Within these endpoints, we have a few

102
00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:01.620
Tim Nunamaker: I would say key parameters that show up a lot.

103
00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:06.879
Tim Nunamaker: Because the… whole data model and the API.

104
00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.619
Tim Nunamaker: It's sort of tailored towards syncing.

105
00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:14.800
Tim Nunamaker: Think, like, Data that is continuously

106
00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:24.850
Tim Nunamaker: fetched in the server, or generated in the server. And some client that wants to, on a regular basis, get the newest information, or the newest data, or the newest records.

107
00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:28.230
Tim Nunamaker: Because that's how the data is sort of modeled.

108
00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:34.579
Tim Nunamaker: Things like cursors are really important in how clients talk to the server, and…

109
00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:37.190
Tim Nunamaker: I'll show some examples here in a second.

110
00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:45.860
Tim Nunamaker: So… we're making a choice, at least for this version of PDPP,

111
00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:52.580
Tim Nunamaker: The spec is fairly narrow in terms of the query API that we're supporting.

112
00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:59.650
Tim Nunamaker: So you can imagine, in an ideal world, if you have access to some data, you can do

113
00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:03.599
Tim Nunamaker: like, really rich queries. You can query for

114
00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:08.489
Tim Nunamaker: Expansions through records into their relationships, like.

115
00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:12.800
Tim Nunamaker: I want to find all of the friends of this person's friends.

116
00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:20.690
Tim Nunamaker: And maybe another layer of friends, and just expanding down. Or you can imagine, really,

117
00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:32.810
Tim Nunamaker: tight control over how records are sorted. I want this… I want to look up all of these contacts sorted by zip code, or even, like, the last four digits of the zip code.

118
00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:37.630
Tim Nunamaker: We're just not supporting, sort of, arbitrarily rich queries in this version.

119
00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:43.880
Tim Nunamaker: One thing that we think clients can do fairly effectively is

120
00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:57.029
Tim Nunamaker: Do an initial query to get some data, or sort of sync the data from the stream that they have access to, and then do re-ranking or additional filtering, or additional sorting the way that they need to.

121
00:17:57.160 --> 00:18:00.660
Tim Nunamaker: I think in the future, we will probably…

122
00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:04.790
Tim Nunamaker: Create some new semantics that make things like expanding relationships.

123
00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:15.540
Tim Nunamaker: Possible by sort of expressing more detail about those relationships within the manifests that the data source authors provide.

124
00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:20.480
Tim Nunamaker: But I think that maybe is a little bit of a stretch at the moment.

125
00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:27.369
Tim Nunamaker: And actually, that being said, there's nothing stopping clients from using

126
00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:33.280
Tim Nunamaker: Supporting additional features. Sorry, nothing stopping servers from supporting additional features.

127
00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:42.439
Tim Nunamaker: So one, maybe, key idea to help understand all of this is when

128
00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:47.839
Tim Nunamaker: Access to data is fulfilled when a client requests access to data.

129
00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:55.360
Tim Nunamaker: The way that the server fulfills that access is actually to go get the grant, and then

130
00:18:55.690 --> 00:19:02.149
Tim Nunamaker: look at the data, grab the data, and filter it through that grant. So the grant is not just

131
00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:03.860
Tim Nunamaker: Sort of metadata.

132
00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:12.199
Tim Nunamaker: It's not just a historical record, it's actively used in the process for returning filtered access to data.

133
00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:19.970
Tim Nunamaker: And yeah, there's probably not much more to say on grants.

134
00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:24.470
Tim Nunamaker: We can talk about…

135
00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:32.449
Tim Nunamaker: how continuous access works. So, let's say once a day or once a week, I have access to a user's

136
00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:39.240
Tim Nunamaker: Listening history in their music app, or I guess in this case, conversations.

137
00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:40.670
Tim Nunamaker: I can ask.

138
00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:45.430
Tim Nunamaker: for all of the newest conversations since the last time I asked.

139
00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:47.360
Tim Nunamaker: And how do I specify that?

140
00:19:47.540 --> 00:20:00.709
Tim Nunamaker: I pass a cursor, which was given to me on my previous request. So this is a way where clients can efficiently sort of ingest the next set of records, across the stream.

141
00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:05.730
Tim Nunamaker: And you'll also notice in this example.

142
00:20:05.930 --> 00:20:18.560
Tim Nunamaker: The second record that was returned is a tombstone. It's basically an indication from the server that a record was deleted. So in the past, maybe as a client, I pulled in a particular conversation.

143
00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:26.170
Tim Nunamaker: And now I'm seeing the user maybe deleted that conversation, or the server decided to delete that conversation for some reason.

144
00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:37.869
Tim Nunamaker: Nothing too complicated here. If you request access for data that you don't have a grant for, you will get back a code basically saying.

145
00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:39.780
Tim Nunamaker: That's not allowed.

146
00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:47.119
Tim Nunamaker: So, I think this is an area in the standard where

147
00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:53.340
Tim Nunamaker: Things may evolve, and we may come back to some of our decisions. There are some open questions.

148
00:20:53.730 --> 00:21:05.129
Tim Nunamaker: Actually… really… tight filters and relationship expansion that we can't support for clients. We can support

149
00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:11.360
Tim Nunamaker: if you own the data. If you are the user, and you're just accessing your own data with

150
00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:17.259
Tim Nunamaker: an owner token. We can't support those things, and we do name them in the core spec.

151
00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:23.350
Tim Nunamaker: So, there's a little bit of a mismatch between what the owner can do and what a client can do.

152
00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:30.830
Tim Nunamaker: like I said, I think in the future, we will expand the sort of power that clients have for querying data.

153
00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:34.450
Tim Nunamaker: Right now, we make a distinction between

154
00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:45.799
Tim Nunamaker: One-shot access, where a grant can only be used once, versus continuous access, but we don't define other kinds of policies about access.

155
00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:48.800
Tim Nunamaker: And… so we may refactor.

156
00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:57.310
Tim Nunamaker: This a little bit to support other kinds of policies, or maybe extensions to the spec can support other kinds of policies.

157
00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:01.950
Tim Nunamaker: or we may choose that single uses.

158
00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:08.520
Tim Nunamaker: Sort of overspecified, and… Leave that up to… implementers.

159
00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:19.509
Tim Nunamaker: When it comes to the process of discovering what data an individual has that can be accessed, we don't have

160
00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:24.309
Tim Nunamaker: like, strong answers for that. The answer is really just…

161
00:22:24.560 --> 00:22:27.850
Tim Nunamaker: The user tells them, at the time of consent.

162
00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:34.969
Tim Nunamaker: So I think I mentioned this earlier, we may add some machinery to make that process a little bit smoother.

163
00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:44.190
Tim Nunamaker: And… Yeah, retention policies.

164
00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:51.519
Tim Nunamaker: And other kinds of policies, which the user is sort of asking the client to commit to as their terms.

165
00:22:51.720 --> 00:23:02.760
Tim Nunamaker: Maybe could be generalized a little bit more, and we can sort of open up the space for other kinds of rules about how the data can be used and what should happen with that.

166
00:23:05.950 --> 00:23:09.760
Tim Nunamaker: A few key decisions that we talked about today.

167
00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:13.270
Tim Nunamaker: Are the grants are immutable, meaning that

168
00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:18.609
Tim Nunamaker: You can always look back in history and see exactly what permission was granted.

169
00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:26.639
Tim Nunamaker: And… How, tokens are enforced is that we use introspection to check

170
00:23:26.860 --> 00:23:30.859
Tim Nunamaker: Sort of, at runtime, whether access is still allowed.

171
00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:37.229
Tim Nunamaker: And our query API is meant to be fairly narrow and small.

172
00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:41.420
Tim Nunamaker: At least in this version of the spec.

173
00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:50.389
Tim Nunamaker: So, this is information we've shared in the past, but if you just drop on our website at PDPP.dev.

174
00:23:50.560 --> 00:24:03.600
Tim Nunamaker: you'll be able to find all of the things, the Discord server, the GitHub. You can build a data connector and try to plug it into a personal server that's also hosted on the website.

175
00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:05.969
Tim Nunamaker: That you can self-host and run yourself.

176
00:24:06.380 --> 00:24:07.500
Tim Nunamaker: And…

177
00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:23.050
Tim Nunamaker: yeah, some of the work still to do is to work out, conformance and building out test suites and making it easier for platforms to conform with the protocol. And then, obviously, this month is all about

178
00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:31.210
Tim Nunamaker: Sort of dotting our… I's and crossing our T's as we get ready to announce this more publicly.

179
00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:36.379
Tim Nunamaker: So I think at this point, I'll just…

180
00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:41.470
Tim Nunamaker: Stop here and open it up for any questions or discussion.

181
00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:50.479
Tim Nunamaker: And we can just… We can just hang on as long as there's stuff to talk about.

182
00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:52.620
Tim Nunamaker: So, yeah, any questions?

183
00:24:54.200 --> 00:25:09.289
Carman Nareau: Yeah, I'll pop in with them. Thanks, Tim, for this. I'm Carman with Mozilla.org. I'm curious if there is a concept of a sub-grant in this, like, where whoever received a grant can pass along a smaller piece of it to someone else, or does every grant always have to come straight from the original data owner?

184
00:25:11.030 --> 00:25:13.609
Tim Nunamaker: I think that's a great question.

185
00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:25.070
Tim Nunamaker: I think as written, the spec basically… Either discourages that, or… Or prohibits it.

186
00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:28.509
Tim Nunamaker: I think we need a better answer for…

187
00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:34.870
Tim Nunamaker: Either the enforceable or unenforceable policies that are expressed By the protocol.

188
00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:40.260
Tim Nunamaker: I think it could be a nice feature… excuse me, feature if users

189
00:25:40.990 --> 00:25:44.669
Tim Nunamaker: Want an application to be able to sort of arbitrarily

190
00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:50.689
Tim Nunamaker: Propagate the access that they're granted in a way that the use… like, if the user trusts some application.

191
00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:52.799
Tim Nunamaker: Weather data.

192
00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:58.119
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, I think that's a good question. I don't think it's a fully resolved question.

193
00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:03.100
Tim Nunamaker: do you have any opinions about how that should work?

194
00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:18.760
Carman Nareau: It's, it's, I ask it because it's something that I don't feel super resolved with, so I was mostly curious your take, if you've, if you've thought about it at all, or, or, yeah, I guess heard from folks how they think about it.

195
00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:24.790
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, I think this is probably something where we think

196
00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:29.549
Tim Nunamaker: There's demand for it, or if the use cases are there.

197
00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:34.570
Tim Nunamaker: we could… I think put some time into figuring out how to solve this.

198
00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:38.960
Tim Nunamaker: I think in this version of the spec.

199
00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:45.770
Tim Nunamaker: it hasn't really been a focus yet, but I'll definitely take that as an open question, and we can…

200
00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:48.300
Tim Nunamaker: Trying to get some clarity on what makes the most sense.

201
00:26:48.810 --> 00:27:03.419
Carman Nareau: Yeah, I appreciate that. And then maybe, like, some tenets I would consider is, like, they're more hunches than anything, but I imagine, like, subgrants would be narrower than the parent, right? You'd always show the full chain back to the original owner and die the moment the parent grant dies.

202
00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:09.580
Carman Nareau: So you don't have this, like, partial survival zombie subgrant out there. But yeah, I appreciate you taking that as an action.

203
00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:12.790
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, absolutely. Lisa?

204
00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:25.840
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Hi, I wanted to know if you're aware of the RAR metadata draft that is going in the… along in the IETF right now.

205
00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:30.390
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Because there seems to be some overlap, and since you already use RAR,

206
00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:39.209
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): I mean, RAR is a RFC, and this is a draft, but Zahavi is, well embedded in the OAuth working group, and

207
00:27:39.390 --> 00:27:41.880
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Has a lot of collaborators.

208
00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:50.680
Tim Nunamaker: Oh, I haven't seen that. Is there, like, a… Two-second summary of, what

209
00:27:51.590 --> 00:27:54.099
Tim Nunamaker: What about that resonates for you?

210
00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:58.620
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Well, I think… What, what resonates?

211
00:27:58.830 --> 00:28:04.470
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): I… Don't know that any of this will be useful for personal data access.

212
00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:12.899
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): So, I wouldn't say it resonates with me, but I just wanted to offer, if it was useful, some…

213
00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:26.149
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): exist… you use a lot of tools that already exist to reduce the amount of work you have to do in PDPP, and this is another piece that would reduce the amount of work you have to do.

214
00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:28.710
Tim Nunamaker: That sounds great.

215
00:28:28.710 --> 00:28:31.500
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Yeah, it's always great.

216
00:28:31.960 --> 00:28:49.070
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Another one is… another, obscure bit of standard stuff that is in RFC is WebDav's report and sync token. This is a lot like cursor, I mean, basically it's a cursor, and allows for synchronizing large collections.

217
00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:52.250
Tim Nunamaker: Nice, okay.

218
00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:56.520
Tim Nunamaker: I'll take that, too, as a note. I think…

219
00:28:57.070 --> 00:29:02.690
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, there are a bunch of, like, cursor-based… protocols out there. We actually.

220
00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:10.110
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): There are a lot. I was making a list of them the other day, like… Starting with… with…

221
00:29:10.240 --> 00:29:19.809
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): rsync, SOAP, Beep, XMPP, Jingle, JMAP, MCP, FTP, Activity, Pub, BitTorrent, GraphQL, Atlas, Atom, AtomPub.

222
00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:21.970
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Solid and web dev.

223
00:29:23.080 --> 00:29:23.670
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Wow.

224
00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:25.750
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

225
00:29:26.490 --> 00:29:31.219
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): This has been a… this is a class of problem that has been tackled

226
00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:50.810
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Many times. Not all of them are as relevant in different situations. Like, the Sync and Resume is not as great fit for ActivityPub, but depending on what use case you have, you can make sync work while using ActivityPub's published stuff, if publishing is more the model.

227
00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:52.440
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): But,

228
00:29:53.130 --> 00:30:10.750
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): We've been trying to get companies with personal data to support the standards that already exist for years, and they prefer to keep it in-house and custom, so that's my biggest. You know, I would love to say lots of this stuff resonates with me, but

229
00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:19.819
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): I don't know how to get any of them to implement a standard for filters and grant scopes and…

230
00:30:21.170 --> 00:30:26.269
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): expirations and… All of that. Pagination, even.

231
00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:27.970
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah.

232
00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:32.910
Tim Nunamaker: I think we started with this… sort of data connector

233
00:30:33.910 --> 00:30:37.200
Tim Nunamaker: Deployment option as a way for us

234
00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:40.679
Tim Nunamaker: to sort of build out an implementation of PDPP.

235
00:30:40.860 --> 00:30:43.479
Tim Nunamaker: Without having to wait for other platforms.

236
00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:54.240
Tim Nunamaker: And in some ways, it seems a little bit… Like, you're cobbling together this… This, like.

237
00:30:54.380 --> 00:31:07.089
Tim Nunamaker: client around an API that may not properly support all of the machinery that the client needs. Yeah. But we've… I think we've been able to get pretty far, surprisingly.

238
00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:14.870
Tim Nunamaker: Across a bunch of different platforms, APIs, and… I think…

239
00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:18.120
Tim Nunamaker: Maybe the eventual answer to this is.

240
00:31:18.940 --> 00:31:21.119
Tim Nunamaker: Sort of along the lines of MCP.

241
00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:28.080
Tim Nunamaker: Like, maybe the way to get people to implement something like this is not to change their existing system.

242
00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:32.410
Tim Nunamaker: But to ship something that can sit on top of it, and maybe it's a little bit…

243
00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:38.580
Tim Nunamaker: Clunky, but if it fulfills the spec, or if it can conform to the standard.

244
00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:45.089
Tim Nunamaker: then… But even if it doesn't conform 100%, maybe there's some optional features that

245
00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:50.320
Tim Nunamaker: are just not supported. Like, the basic… Get the data out.

246
00:31:50.750 --> 00:31:51.940
Tim Nunamaker: like, feature.

247
00:31:52.100 --> 00:31:54.380
Tim Nunamaker: I think that's…

248
00:31:54.730 --> 00:32:02.060
Tim Nunamaker: that's where I'm putting some of my… my optimism and hope in that direction. Like, there's some kind of bridge to what exists today, but…

249
00:32:02.270 --> 00:32:03.730
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, I would love to hear it.

250
00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:10.839
Tim Nunamaker: Any other thoughts you have about, how to get these guys to move, in this direction?

251
00:32:11.290 --> 00:32:11.830
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Yep.

252
00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:16.250
Tim Nunamaker: Hmm.

253
00:32:18.730 --> 00:32:21.650
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, and I think along those lines,

254
00:32:23.210 --> 00:32:27.920
Tim Nunamaker: There are a number of different ways that you can imagine accessing data.

255
00:32:28.150 --> 00:32:32.369
Tim Nunamaker: We're talking here about… Syncing data periodically.

256
00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:44.339
Tim Nunamaker: if you look at something like a PDPA export, like a Google takeout style, just export everything. We haven't put a lot of focus on stuff like that because

257
00:32:44.650 --> 00:32:48.749
Tim Nunamaker: If all the data is in the server, an export shouldn't be that hard to build.

258
00:32:48.990 --> 00:32:53.349
Tim Nunamaker: Versus a real-time query API, so we started with the harder thing.

259
00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:57.760
Tim Nunamaker: But… There may be other… Hmm…

260
00:32:58.230 --> 00:33:05.510
Tim Nunamaker: Access paths or use cases where we can… Maybe define, like.

261
00:33:06.260 --> 00:33:14.590
Tim Nunamaker: more specifically, here's how it works, and here's why it… here's how it can work well. Maybe, like, additional APIs that we don't have in the spec right now.

262
00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:18.600
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah.

263
00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:23.680
Tim Nunamaker: Any other questions or comments?

264
00:33:27.440 --> 00:33:27.990
Carman Nareau: Yeah, cool.

265
00:33:28.690 --> 00:33:30.089
Maciej: Yeah, go on, Carman.

266
00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:31.539
Carman Nareau: Go ahead, I've gone.

267
00:33:32.360 --> 00:33:34.789
Maciej: Okay, I've got a question,

268
00:33:35.100 --> 00:33:41.190
Maciej: do you… do you think, Tim, or maybe you thought about, like, how much protocol should be opinionated about

269
00:33:41.310 --> 00:33:50.909
Maciej: approving grants and building features related to grant approvals. I'm thinking about things like Delegating approval of grants.

270
00:33:51.190 --> 00:33:56.010
Maciej: you know, for example, to AI Agent, or something like that.

271
00:33:56.160 --> 00:34:14.039
Maciej: Right? Basically, assuming that I will not be always available to approve a grant, I would like to have maybe automation in an auditable way set up that I can… and maybe a few automations, and I would be, you know, and maybe set some policies about, you know, what kind of data should be approved, and so on.

272
00:34:15.159 --> 00:34:18.089
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, great question.

273
00:34:18.399 --> 00:34:21.749
Tim Nunamaker: I think… A few weeks ago.

274
00:34:22.779 --> 00:34:30.029
Tim Nunamaker: the way that the spec was written was more tightly coupled to OAuth as an authorization protocol.

275
00:34:30.199 --> 00:34:33.229
Tim Nunamaker: And we actually realized we could…

276
00:34:33.379 --> 00:34:44.639
Tim Nunamaker: refactor that a little bit, and make it more flexible. And so, in the future, we could potentially support something like, GNAP, GNAP.

277
00:34:44.789 --> 00:34:46.079
Tim Nunamaker: And I think…

278
00:34:46.209 --> 00:34:58.029
Tim Nunamaker: There's nothing really about PDPP that says you can't implement an authorization protocol that makes use of an AI agent, or some other undefined mechanism.

279
00:34:58.339 --> 00:35:01.919
Tim Nunamaker: I think it could be a really interesting

280
00:35:02.119 --> 00:35:04.489
Tim Nunamaker: avenue to explore, and I think…

281
00:35:05.359 --> 00:35:08.139
Tim Nunamaker: with AI agents getting more sophisticated.

282
00:35:08.349 --> 00:35:11.149
Tim Nunamaker: And users potentially delegating more and more.

283
00:35:11.439 --> 00:35:17.219
Tim Nunamaker: And… and potentially establishing a lot of trust. Like, if you have…

284
00:35:17.479 --> 00:35:24.589
Tim Nunamaker: Maybe some lower-stakes data, and you don't want to always have to be in the loop to approve requests, but you also don't want to just

285
00:35:24.999 --> 00:35:28.019
Tim Nunamaker: Automatically click a button. Maybe some agent can help.

286
00:35:28.189 --> 00:35:35.339
Tim Nunamaker: mediate that process. Yeah, I think we want there to be ways for… Implementers to support that.

287
00:35:35.649 --> 00:35:45.929
Tim Nunamaker: Given that like, PDPP is really just about the language of data portability, We're not intending to…

288
00:35:46.649 --> 00:35:49.369
Tim Nunamaker: Prevent, use cases like that.

289
00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:53.200
Maciej: Cool, thank you.

290
00:35:59.200 --> 00:36:00.830
Tim Nunamaker: Cool, any other questions?

291
00:36:03.670 --> 00:36:19.660
Carman Nareau: Yeah, I was just, kind of noodling, like, from a policy perspective, what happens if the, whatever, the thing I've given permission to changes legal ownership? You know, does my… do I have to… does that,

292
00:36:20.060 --> 00:36:26.840
Carman Nareau: prompt me to revoke my access, because it's a new relationship with a new, kind of, company or corporate structure.

293
00:36:27.250 --> 00:36:36.809
Carman Nareau: you know, I think it could be interesting to have clients have to sort of report that type of ownership, like there was a security issue, but just something to… I'm curious if you've thought about at all.

294
00:36:37.390 --> 00:36:40.470
Tim Nunamaker: Hmm. Do you think you could give me a specific example?

295
00:36:41.530 --> 00:36:50.720
Carman Nareau: Yeah, sure. So, you know, if I'm giving access… or sorry, if I'm, giving permission

296
00:36:51.030 --> 00:37:08.000
Carman Nareau: for something to… yeah, sorry, if I'm giving read permission to a certain set of information, or to a client for a certain set of information, I'm probably making a certain assumption about the relationship I have with that company, right? So maybe… let's just take Spotify. I'm giving Spotify read access.

297
00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:13.639
Carman Nareau: If Spotify were to, like, change legal structures or get absorbed into something else, like.

298
00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:20.030
Carman Nareau: I would want the option to know that, to be able to revoke, because it would be a different…

299
00:37:20.270 --> 00:37:23.719
Carman Nareau: like, it's a different company now. Does that make sense?

300
00:37:24.640 --> 00:37:25.180
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah.

301
00:37:25.510 --> 00:37:26.670
Tim Nunamaker: So it's basically, like.

302
00:37:26.670 --> 00:37:32.719
Carman Nareau: It's a little bit terms of service or privacy policy, like, adjacent, but I guess, like, within the structure.

303
00:37:34.780 --> 00:37:41.040
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, I think… I mean, I think this is a really interesting question. It's like, If a user has

304
00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:47.069
Tim Nunamaker: specific terms that they want to be attached to their data. How can those be expressed in…

305
00:37:47.190 --> 00:37:55.160
Tim Nunamaker: to the extent possible, enforced by the protocol. Obviously, there are some limits there, but…

306
00:37:57.930 --> 00:38:03.120
Tim Nunamaker: I think I'll have to think some more about that. Right now, it wouldn't be hard to extend

307
00:38:03.330 --> 00:38:10.300
Tim Nunamaker: The standard to allow for Sort of user-specified terms. And then maybe…

308
00:38:10.520 --> 00:38:17.580
Tim Nunamaker: The authorization server could have some templates or some recipes for, like, these are things that our users typically

309
00:38:18.100 --> 00:38:22.280
Tim Nunamaker: Want to request, or maybe that could just be…

310
00:38:22.570 --> 00:38:27.520
Tim Nunamaker: some other RFC that kind of lays out

311
00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:30.030
Tim Nunamaker: What… what those common things are.

312
00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:36.790
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, I think that's a good question. I think there's probably a gap.

313
00:38:37.150 --> 00:38:40.569
Tim Nunamaker: When it comes to… fully addressing

314
00:38:40.710 --> 00:38:45.890
Tim Nunamaker: Like, what all the different things that the user could want when it comes to granting access to their data.

315
00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:48.510
Tim Nunamaker: So, yeah, I'll take a look at that for sure.

316
00:38:48.750 --> 00:39:01.719
Carman Nareau: Yeah, and I appreciate that being zoomed out, because it could include also stuff like the client outsources the data processing to, like, a third party that was, like, previously undisclosed, or any… any list of… of things, so, yeah, thanks.

317
00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:04.710
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, Lisa?

318
00:39:05.940 --> 00:39:17.660
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): Yeah, the, this is mostly in response to Carman. The, the… are you aware of the ISO standard for my terms? This would cover the bulk of user data

319
00:39:17.660 --> 00:39:28.000
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): hosting by the direct, like, first party, rather than third-party access, the first-party holding of bulk data, which users currently have no control over the terms of.

320
00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:31.089
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): So that covers a large surface area.

321
00:39:31.780 --> 00:39:33.620
Carman Nareau: Oh, cool, and you said it's called My Terms?

322
00:39:33.620 --> 00:39:34.860
Lisa Dusseault (Data Transfer Initiative): It's called My Terms, yep.

323
00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:35.410
Carman Nareau: Okay.

324
00:39:43.360 --> 00:39:44.999
Tim Nunamaker: Alright, any other questions?

325
00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:55.519
Tim Nunamaker: I think we can wrap up this session,

326
00:39:56.580 --> 00:40:03.929
Tim Nunamaker: Yeah, just a reminder, Art will be leading the next one next week. I think the time will be a little bit later in the day. He's in Australia.

327
00:40:04.190 --> 00:40:08.029
Tim Nunamaker: But he'll definitely have less…

328
00:40:08.280 --> 00:40:16.860
Tim Nunamaker: material on the technical side, and more on the governance and policy side, which I think could be really interesting. So, hope to see you all there.

329
00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:20.859
Tim Nunamaker: Thank you for joining, thanks for all the great questions and feedback.

330
00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:25.560
Tim Nunamaker: And I don't know who's gonna be in JC, but if you're there, we should definitely…

331
00:40:25.670 --> 00:40:30.909
Tim Nunamaker: sync up, and… yeah, I'm excited. So thanks, everyone.

